{"id":"43e1d5691112563a40fcb50daf3336a1c2fb2559f54902aaff1a0385eb8b54aa","lamport":0,"author":"scual.project@gmail.com","created_at":"2026-09-27T11:00:38.581800055Z","format_version":1,"kind":"create","payload":{"fields":{"created-at":"2026-09-27T11:00:38.581800055Z","created-by":"scual.project@gmail.com","description":"## Problem Statement\n\n`ti list` has two filters for exploring Dependencies between tickets, `--depends X` and `--blocked-by X`. Their names mislead users:\n\n- `--blocked-by X` returns the tickets that **block** X (X's blockers), the exact opposite of what the name reads as (\"tickets blocked by X\").\n- `--depends X` is ambiguous: it could mean \"tickets that depend on X\" or \"tickets X depends on\". (It actually means the former.)\n- The two flags *sound* like synonyms but return opposite ends of the relation.\n- Neither name matches the vocabulary the rest of TicGit uses: the relation is created with `ti depends` and shown in `ti show` as \"Depends\" / \"Blocks\".\n\nOn top of that:\n\n- Both filters silently hide blockers or dependents that are sub-issues (because `ti list` hides sub-issues by default). A user asking \"what blocks X?\" can get an empty list when an open sub-issue is in fact blocking X — a false \"nothing is blocking\" answer.\n- Saved views store whatever ticket reference the user typed (often a short prefix), which can become ambiguous as tickets accumulate.\n- The CLI integration test for `--blocked-by` is currently failing: it invokes a `ti blocked-by` subcommand that does not exist.\n\n## Solution\n\nRename the filters so each one reads as a predicate on the listed tickets and mirrors the domain vocabulary (see CONTEXT.md: **Dependency**, **Depends on**, **Blocks**):\n\n- `ti list --depends-on X` — tickets that depend on X (downstream: who waits on X).\n- `ti list --blocks X` — tickets that block X (upstream: who X waits on).\n\nThe old `--depends` and `--blocked-by` flags are removed outright (no aliases). When either new flag is given, sub-issues are always included. The open-only default still applies, so by default only tickets that are *currently* blocking/waiting are shown; `--all` includes closed ones. Relations are direct only. The ticket argument is required. Saved views pin the ticket by its full id.\n\nGiven `ti depends B -t A` (A depends on B, B blocks A):\n\n| Command | Result |\n|---|---|\n| `ti list --depends-on B` | A |\n| `ti list --blocks A` | B |\n| `ti list --depends-on A` | (nothing) |\n| `ti list --blocks B` | (nothing) |\n\n## User Stories\n\n1. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --blocks X` to list the tickets that block X, so that I can see what I must finish before X can proceed.\n2. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --depends-on X` to list the tickets that depend on X, so that I can see what finishing X will unblock.\n3. As a TicGit user, I want the flag names to match the \"Depends\" and \"Blocks\" labels in `ti show`, so that I can predict a flag's result from what a ticket displays.\n4. As a TicGit user, I want the flag names to match the `ti depends` command vocabulary, so that I only have to learn one word per direction.\n5. As a TicGit user, I want `--depends-on X` to return exactly the tickets whose \"Depends\" list contains X, so that the filter behaves as a literal lookup.\n6. As a TicGit user, I want `--blocks X` to return exactly the tickets whose \"Blocks\" list contains X, so that the filter behaves as a literal lookup.\n7. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --blocks X` to include blockers that are sub-issues, so that I never wrongly conclude X is unblocked.\n8. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --depends-on X` to include dependents that are sub-issues, so that I see every ticket X is holding up.\n9. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --blocks X` to show only open blockers by default, so that I see only what is still actually blocking X (consistent with `ti next`).\n10. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --blocks X --all` to include closed blockers, so that I can review the full history of X's dependencies.\n11. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --depends-on X --all` to include closed dependents, so that I can see everything that ever waited on X.\n12. As a TicGit user, I want the relationship filters to return only direct relations, so that results match the stored relation and `ti next`'s notion of \"unblocked\".\n13. As a TicGit user, I want to pass X as a full id or a unique prefix, so that I can type short references like other commands allow.\n14. As a TicGit user, I want a clear error when X does not resolve to a ticket (unknown or ambiguous prefix), so that I don't get a silently empty list.\n15. As a TicGit user, I want `--depends-on` and `--blocks` to require a value, so that `ti list --blocks <view-name>` is never misread.\n16. As a TicGit user, I want `--depends-on` and `--blocks` to combine with other filters (tag, assignee, state, search, order) using AND semantics, so that I can narrow results, e.g. \"open bugs blocking X\".\n17. As a TicGit user, I want to combine `--depends-on X` and `--blocks Y` in one query, so that I can find tickets sitting between two others in a chain.\n18. As a TicGit user who used the old flags, I want `--depends` and `--blocked-by` to fail with an \"unexpected argument\" error, so that I notice the rename instead of getting silently different results.\n19. As a TicGit user, I want `ti list --help` to describe each relationship flag in terms of Depends on / Blocks, so that the help text is unambiguous.\n20. As a TicGit user, I want to save a view that includes `--blocks X` or `--depends-on X`, so that I can re-run a relationship query by name.\n21. As a TicGit user, I want a saved view to pin the ticket by its full id, so that the view keeps pointing at the same ticket even after new tickets make my typed prefix ambiguous.\n22. As a TicGit user, I want `ti views list` to describe relationship filters using the new flag names, so that the description matches what I would type.\n23. As a TicGit user, I want `--json` and `--markdown` output to work unchanged with the new filters, so that scripts and agents can consume results.\n24. As an AI agent using TicGit, I want unambiguous relationship flags, so that I can reliably determine which tickets block the one I'm working on before starting.\n25. As a maintainer, I want the CLI test suite to pass, so that the broken `ti blocked-by` test no longer masks regressions.\n26. As a maintainer, I want test fixtures named after their role (blocker, dependent), so that tests document the relation direction correctly.\n\n## Implementation Decisions\n\n- **Vocabulary**: Follow CONTEXT.md. A **Dependency** is one directed relation read two ways: \"A depends on B\" ≡ \"B blocks A\". \"Blocked by\" is an avoided term and must not appear in flags, fields or help text.\n- **CLI (`ti list`)**: Replace the `--depends` flag with `--depends-on <TICKET>` and the `--blocked-by` flag with `--blocks <TICKET>`. Both take a required value (id or unique prefix), resolved through the store's existing id resolution. No aliases for the old names; clap's default \"unexpected argument\" error is the migration signal.\n- **Filter struct (query module)**: Rename the fields `depends` → `depends_on` and `blocked_by` → `blocks` (both `Option<Uuid>`). Semantics unchanged: `depends_on: Some(X)` keeps tickets whose depends-on set contains X; `blocks: Some(X)` keeps tickets whose blocks set contains X. Direct relations only — no transitive walk.\n- **Sub-issue bypass**: In the filter application, when `depends_on` or `blocks` is set, the hide-sub-issues rule is not applied. The status/state defaults (open-only unless `--all`, `--status` or `--state`) are unchanged and still apply.\n- **Saved views / last filters**: Rename the saved-view fields `depends` → `depends_on` and `blocked_by` → `blocks`. Store the ticket's full UUID (resolved at `ti list` time, before the last filters are recorded) instead of the raw string the user typed. Old keys (`depends`, `blocked_by`) are not migrated; they are ignored on load. Loading a saved view resolves the stored full id as usual.\n- **View description**: The saved-view description shows `--depends-on <id>` / `--blocks <id>`. It may shorten the UUID for display.\n- **TUI**: Only mechanical updates for the renamed fields (the TUI keeps passing \"none\" for both). No new TUI capability.\n- **Help text**: `--depends-on`: \"Show tickets that depend on this ticket.\" `--blocks`: \"Show tickets that block this ticket.\"\n- **Rename only**: No change to how Dependencies are stored or created (`ti depends`), and no change to `ti show` rendering.\n\n## Testing Decisions\n\n- Good tests assert externally observable behaviour: which ticket ids come back from `ti list --json` for a given set of Dependencies and flags. They do not inspect state files or internal structs beyond the public `Filter` / `apply` interface.\n- **Primary seam — CLI integration tests** (existing `TestRepo` harness, prior art: the existing `list_depends_filters_dependent_tickets` and saved-view tests in the CLI test suite). Cover:\n  - `--depends-on B` returns A; `--blocks A` returns B; the reversed queries return nothing.\n  - A sub-issue blocker is returned by `--blocks X` without `--subissues`; likewise for `--depends-on`.\n  - A closed blocker is hidden by default and shown with `--all`.\n  - `--depends` and `--blocked-by` fail with a non-zero exit.\n  - Unknown ticket reference fails with a non-zero exit.\n  - Save/replay: `ti list --blocks <prefix>` → `ti views save v` → `ti list v` returns the same tickets; `ti views list` shows `--blocks` with the full id.\n  - Replace the broken `list_blocked_by_filters_blocked_tickets` test (which calls a non-existent `ti blocked-by` subcommand) with a correct `--blocks` test that creates the Dependency via `ti depends`.\n- **Secondary seam — `query::apply` unit tests** (prior art: existing filter tests in the query module, e.g. `filter_by_dependency`). Cover the literal field lookup for both filters and the sub-issue bypass. Rename the misleading fixture: the ticket inserted with a `blocks` entry is the **blocker**, not \"blocked\".\n- No new seams introduced.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- Transitive / recursive dependency queries (e.g. \"everything upstream of X\"). Better served by a future tree or graph view; to be filed as a separate ticket.\n- A CLI-wide \"current ticket\" sentinel (e.g. `--blocks .`) or defaulting the value to the checked-out ticket. To be filed as a separate ticket.\n- Exposing relationship filters in the TUI.\n- Backward-compatibility aliases or migration of old saved-view keys.\n- Changes to how Dependencies are created, stored, or rendered.\n- Repeatable flags (`--blocks X --blocks Y`).\n\n## Further Notes\n\n- The original commits (a35c101, 84c2d7c) are on master but in no tagged release, which is why the old flags are removed without deprecation.\n- The existing filter logic is already correct for the new names: current `--depends` logic becomes `--depends-on`; current `--blocked-by` logic becomes `--blocks`.\n- Domain terms are recorded in CONTEXT.md (Dependency, Depends on, Blocks).","state":"review","status":"open","title":"Rename `ti list` dependency filters to --depends-on / --blocks"}}}